{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1553137-higher-difficulty-vs-more-successes","id":1553137,"title":"Higher Difficulty vs More Successes","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-08-13T08:36:05-07:00","id":1553137,"title":"Higher Difficulty vs More Successes","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Higher Difficulty vs More Successes<br /> <br /> (Sorry in advance if this has already been discussed before),</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">In the Storyteller System (which I appreciate very much), I always had a hard time to understand the difference between raising the difficulty of a roll vs requiring more successes on a roll.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I have the following books: M20, W20, C20 and V20.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">They all have the same explanations (mostly) in their section where they explain how the rule system works.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">But to me, it looks like we have a choice to make when a simple roll (difficulty 6, 1 success required) is not what would properly represent the difficulty of a task to be executed.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Can anyone explain to me how the system is supposed to be used? If not too much to ask, maybe some examples to compare:</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><u>-Why a roll needs a higher difficulty</u></span><br /> <br /> <u><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">-Why a roll needs more successes</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">-Why a roll needs a higher difficulty AND more successes.</span></u><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">(While mentioning more successes, I am not referring to extended rolls over an extended period of time. I understand that part of the rules.)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Also, regarding a too high difficulty (9 and 10), I am aware that the way the system works makes the probability of failing and botching higher.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Also, I really like what C20 proposed: to make a roll including some 1s is a botch only if the roll has no success at all (C20 p.245):</span><br /> <br /> <i><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u201c Optional Rule: Fear of Failure</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Sometimes players fail rolls a little too often. An occasional botch keeps a game interesting. Repeated botches risk compounding a feeling of failure, and can freeze a plot if dice rolls prevent characters from moving forward.</span></i><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><i>If you find players are failing rolls too much and you want a slightly more forgiving take on the system, remove the rule of 1s cancelling successes. 1s now only count as botches where zero successes appear. Players may spend a Willpower point to remove the chance of botching. \u201c</i></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Thanks in advance for you time!</span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25337-lightbringer1","name":"Lightbringer1","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25337_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-13T21:21:15-07:00","id":1553159,"title":"","content":"<u><b>Higher Difficulty</b>:</u> it usually means the task has a higher failure risk. With a high difficulty both the chances of no successes and Botch are higher. Usually a task that's harder should go for this option as a default;<br /> <br /> <b><u>More Successes</u></b><u>:</u> it usually means the task has more <u>granularity</u>. Any success is still a success, so the character did accomplish <i>something</i>, but not necessarily to the degree they aimed for. You should ask for more successes when you plan on breaking down the result according to the number of successes achieved. In fact, the default isn't 1 success <i>exactly</i>, but actually 3, with 1 succeeding with complications and 5 going far beyond expectations. Usually a task that requires more successes to accomplish has still some effect when it fails with a lower number of successes instead of no success;<br /> <br /> <u><b>Both</b>:</u> it just means both things apply. Sometimes it's a way to not increase one aspect too much, so you increase successes for a particularly hard task instead of making it too high Difficulty. It may also apply when one is defined by a given aspect of the task, but the other by another. Lets say a character wants to jump over a large hole with slippery edges, you may decide the length of the jump defines successes needed, but the slipperiness defines Difficulty. Most of the time, though, those will be rolls called by supernatural powers with specific rules, so you don't really have to keep it in mind, any power with such a roll will explicitly say so;<br /> <br /> <u><b>Optional rules</b>:</u> the rule of not counting it as a Botch unless there's no successes to begin with is a very good one. You'll quickly notice with actual play that otherwise Botches are very common, and their impact on the game is quite big. But it is still a valid play style a lot of people enjoy. I think using this optional rule is actually a better starting point until you get the hang of it and decide if you want more Botches or not, as Botches not only have a high impact on the game, they also require a lot of improvisation you may either welcome or find excessive.<br /> <br /> Another rule to consider, that usually appears in Mage, is the <i>Threshold Rule</i>. In it, any time a Difficulty rises above 9 you low it back to 9, but takes the difference and deduces from the successes achieved. So a Dif 10 task loses the first success, a dif 11 task loses 2 successes, and so on. This isn't common to come about, but it's there to consider. I personally use an <i>Inverted Threshold Rule</i> in my own Mage games, giving extra successes when the difficulty would otherwise be lower than 3, and it works for me, but that's open for debate by the community, use at your own peril.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-13T21:40:16-07:00","id":1553162,"title":"","content":"Ultimately? No, we can't directly address your question. One of the downsides of the classic ST system is that it never really codified what different bonuses/penalties are meant to do. When's a dice penalty? A difficulty increase? An increased number of successes? Basically ST vibes. Some specific rules (magic casting in Mage) have specifics, but they're not very prone to generalize from.<br /> <br /> The only real thing that helps with diff vs. more successes is successes can be incomplete/partial. Three successes could be ideal, but one success on the roll could be a partial success. So if you want &quot;difficulty&quot; that's more about how cleanly the PC(s) succeed at a task instead of if they do at all, increasing successes is basically the way to go. Difficulty is more useful for whether succeeding at all is harder.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-14T02:24:46-07:00","id":1553164,"title":"","content":"In very broad terms, I would say the difficulty is the threshold you need to clear in order to be successful at all, while the number of successes indicates the <i>quality</i> of what you do. Sometimes you need to perform a task at a certain quality threshold, which is when you use both.<br /> <br /> If your character strikes someone, a single success means the strike lands. If there are extra successes, it was a good strike and it might translate into more damage. If the target has soak, you need that extra damage (quality threshold) so the target feels it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-08-27T10:40:46-07:00","id":1553486,"title":"","content":"Difficulty represents how hard it is to accomplish a task with any level of success, while successes represent how well you succeed. In the end, these are all various levers to pull and play with.<ul><li><b>The GM may give the character bonus or penalty dice.</b> This is best used when the altering factor is coming from within the character, such as a momentary burst of strength or a bleeding wound.</li> <li><b>The GM may increase or decrease the number of successes required.</b> This is best used to represent when you need to know how well they succeeded and where a marginal completion of a task isn't enough. For example, making a sword isn't hard, but making a high quality sword requires a lot of skill, having multiple successes needed to create a high quality sword compared to a single success to make a basic sword.</li> <li><b>The GM can alter the value needed for a success.</b> This may seem unnecessary, it allows for scenarios in which the total potential of success does not change, but the likelihood of success does. If a character scored all their dice as successes, this represents their maximum capability, and so adjusting the value needed for determines how easy it for that potential to come out on a task.</li> </ul>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1VN49Y7tH2ZV0l7Hx65RWX1ez-Q9Lst-8ZTyTJeCTpxM/\" target=\"_blank\">Sorcerer 20th</a> homebrew and my update to <a href=\"http://immortalthegathering.blogspot.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Highlander: the Gathering</a> for 20th Anniversary edition.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2414-theoutlander523","name":"theoutlander523","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}